 <!--[That guy playing bass is absolutely going to make it big.]

In hindsight, Zhou Huai would come to suspect that this message had been nothing but a spectacular jinx. Less than three seconds after receiving it, the livehouse plunged into darkness, bringing the performance to an abrupt halt.

The heat and exhilaration of moments ago were swallowed up in an instant, as though sucked into a black hole. Silence descended. Everyone's excitement seemed to hang suspended in a vacuum.

"Shit. Did the power just go out?"

The voice shattered the silence, and a wave of restless chatter immediately swept through the crowd.

It wasn't just the electricity that had gone out. The future of the new band onstage had just been cut short, too.

At least, that's what Zhou Huai thought.

"The wristband lights are out, too. Does that mean our votes don't count anymore? They'll come back when the power does, right?"

"What if they don't? We only get three votes for the preliminaries! I still need to vote for my favourite bands!"

"If they don't come back, then we just don't vote again. What can you do? They're just unlucky."

For a live performance, atmosphere was everything. Once the spark was extinguished and the momentum broken, not even a miracle could bring it back.

Worse still, all the votes that would determine the band's fate had now been wiped clean.

Talk about bad luck.

The enclosed livehouse had become a stuffy, pitch-black box. Complaints piled on top of one another, voices trampling over voices, making the already suffocating atmosphere even more unbearable.

The guy from the message—the one playing bass—was still standing onstage. Compared to his two bandmates, he looked almost like an outsider, completely unfazed by what had happened. One hand rested around the neck of his bass, while the other was casually draped over the microphone stand. His fingers were even tapping out a leisurely rhythm.

A few flashlight beams flickered on at the side of the stage, presumably from staff investigating the outage. Their narrow, faint beams swept across the musicians, never quite illuminating their faces. Even so, their silhouettes were striking enough.

With talent and looks like his, it was hard to imagine him not making it big. Just like Qin Yiyu had once been.

What a shame. He'd run into circumstances beyond anyone's control. If they couldn't even get past the preliminaries, the band's future was as good as over.

Suddenly, the hand resting on the microphone stand lifted slightly. The guy moved his fingers towards the restless crowd below, almost like a wave, though the gesture carried something vaguely ambiguous.

And the person he was gesturing towards...

Seemed to be standing right beside Zhou Huai.

Even while listening to the performance earlier, Zhou Huai had found himself thinking something.

The guy was quiet, hardly speaking at all. But his hands, much like his eyes, seemed capable of saying things words couldn't.

It reminded Zhou Huai of the first time he'd encountered him, five days earlier.

That day, he'd gone to the post office to collect a letter. On his way back, he'd received a phone call, so he'd spent the drive to the tattoo shop joking around and talking nonsense.

"It's not a debt collector, but someone's already tracked you down to your doorstep..."

When he was almost there, he squeezed his car into a gap at the entrance of an alley, pulled out the letter, and skimmed through it. His heart sank a little. Without saying another word, he shoved it back into the envelope and tucked it away in the centre console.

To avoid giving anything away, he made his voice considerably more exaggerated than usual.

"Well, sounds like you've got yourself a stalker!"

He got out of the car, only to be greeted by an intense wave of sweetness.

"Whoa. Those roasted sweet potatoes smell amazing."

He quickly returned to the conversation, his tone turning teasing and obnoxious.

"Don't tell me it's some lovesick girl? Still pining after you after all this time?"

The person on the other end swore at him, making Zhou Huai laugh even harder. When he looked up, he spotted a little roadside stall. But the moment he got a proper look at the person running it, his steps faltered.

"Fuck."

"Since when did guys this handsome start selling sweet potatoes on the street?"

An elderly man with a slight limp was making his way towards the stall, so Zhou Huai stepped aside and waited behind him, taking the opportunity to size up the handsome young man in front of him.

The guy was unusually quiet. A customer had approached, and he hadn't said a single word.

He was dressed entirely in black, with a dark grey baseball cap pulled low over his forehead, concealing half his face with ease. The jacket was nothing more than an old biker jacket, yet somehow it looked ridiculously good on him. He had the proportions of a model: a slim waist, long legs, and an effortlessly striking appearance.

His hair was quite long, too.

When he lowered his head, Zhou Huai caught sight of a little ponytail tied at the back of his head, along with a row of silver earrings running up his right ear. The late-summer sunlight glinted off them.

Zhou Huai was so busy admiring the view that he suddenly realised the elderly man hadn't said anything either.

His mouth was open, but instead of speaking, he was gesturing with his hands, looking rather troubled.

The handsome young man with the perpetually cold expression watched him for a moment before reaching into his pocket and, to Zhou Huai's surprise, responding fluently in sign language.

"What the fuck?"

The person on the phone still hadn't hung up. His voice came through lazily.

[What happened? Did that sweet potato grow legs and run off with the handsome guy?]

"Well, I'll be damned." Now that he knew the guy couldn't hear him, Zhou Huai didn't bother lowering his voice. He sighed. "He's handsome and deaf. What a waste."

The elderly man paid and left. Zhou Huai pointed towards the oven, held up one finger, then paused and gestured for the other person to wait a moment as he turned back to the phone.

"Hey, Qin Yiyu. Want one?"

He failed to notice that the handsome young man in front of him had suddenly raised his head.

"No? Fine. Don't come begging for a bite later."

Zhou Huai pursed his lips and was just about to repeat his gesture when a middle-aged man came hurrying over from a short distance away, panting as he repeatedly thanked the young man running the stall.

"Thank you so much, young man! Really, thank you for watching the stall for me. There've been so many tourists in this alley lately. Even going to the bathroom means standing in a queue!"

The supposedly "deaf" handsome guy replied quietly, "It's no trouble."

Fuck.

He could talk?!

And his voice was ridiculously good, too!

"The owner's back."

He dropped the words casually and turned to leave.

"You can buy from him."

...

Zhou Huai had never been this embarrassed in his entire life.

While he was still standing there in a daze, Qin Yiyu's voice suddenly took on a more serious edge in his earphones, tinged with irritation.

[That fat bastard's here causing trouble again. Don't come back to the shop yet.]

[Save me a bite of that sweet potato.]

After hanging up, Qin Yiyu rubbed his temples, then put on a bright, shameless smile and leaned over the counter, waving at the local thug.

"Good morning!"

"Back for another visit? Huai's not here right now. Why don't you come back another day?"

"Like hell I'm here for a visit!"

The fat man yanked at his collar and launched into a string of vicious curses, dragging Qin Yiyu's ancestors into it for good measure. But when it came down to it, it was the same old story: the tattoo was botched, he wanted compensation, and if Qin Yiyu didn't pay up, he wasn't going anywhere.

How many times was this going to happen this month?

The shop was already struggling to attract customers. At this rate, everything they earned wouldn't even cover what this bastard was extorting from them.

Qin Yiyu flashed him a grin.

"What's wrong with it? Let me have a look."

"Right here! Take a good look yourself!"

He actually pulled his shirt open?

Qin Yiyu narrowed his eyes. Looking at that was practically an assault on his vision.

"Well, how should I put this..."

Leaning against the counter, he smiled pleasantly.

"You do realise a drawing on paper and a drawing on a slab of pork aren't going to look exactly the same, right? It's close enough. Cut me some slack, would you?"

The fat man exploded.

"What the fuck is wrong with you?!"

Who would've thought Qin Yiyu would happily play along?

"Exactly! You've got me figured out! And you could tell just by looking? Finally, someone who understands me!"

He grabbed the fat man's hand and shook it enthusiastically.

The fat man jerked his hand away in fury, snatched up a bottle of tattoo ink from beside the counter, and swung it straight at Qin Yiyu's head.

"I'LL FUCKING KILL YOU!"

What a temper.

Qin Yiyu couldn't even be bothered to dodge. He didn't so much as lift an eyelid. He'd already prepared himself to take the first hit, collapse onto the floor, and play dead while making a scene.

People who knew how to fight understood better than anyone where to hit someone without actually killing them.

But the glass bottle never came crashing down as expected.

Had his hangover gotten so bad that even his sense of pain was malfunctioning?

"You motherfucker, who the hell are—"

Hm?

Qin Yiyu finally lifted his eyelids.

The fat man's greasy hand had stopped midair, firmly caught in a pale hand with prominent tendons and well-defined knuckles.

Before the man could finish speaking, he was suddenly shoved backwards. He staggered several steps, like a crumbling wall on the verge of collapse, and just as he struggled to regain his balance, another kick came flying straight at him.

"Fuck!"

At first glance, the kick didn't even look particularly forceful. Yet the fat man went tumbling to the ground, his back hitting the floor with a loud crash. His fleshy face twisted into a grimace.

Pain exploded through his stomach, and his head rang. Before he could even get back up, the other person strode towards him, bent down, grabbed him by the collar, and dragged him bodily out of the shop with one hand.

He made it look easier than dragging a dog.

Qin Yiyu couldn't help raising an eyebrow.

That kind of efficiency was straight out of a movie about a psychopathic serial killer.

Had he not recognised the person immediately, even he would've been startled.

The fat man sat sprawled on the ground, seeing stars. His neck was red from where his collar had been caught. It took him several seconds to gather his bearings, and the moment he did, he started cursing.

"What the fuck are you supposed to be?! I'll fucking—"

He didn't get to finish.

The moment he saw the other person's foot lift again, the fat man immediately backed down. He instinctively raised his hands to shield himself and, at long last, shut his mouth.

The kick never came.

The foot simply settled back onto the ground.

The local thug, having failed to extort any money, grabbed the doorframe and struggled to his feet. He shot a glance over his shoulder, still wanting to hurl a few more insults at Qin Yiyu over the intimidating man's shoulder.

After all, Qin Yiyu was standing there grinning and waving at him with an utterly infuriating expression.

But the next second, the person in front of him tilted his head slightly, blocking his view.

They were close. For the first time, he found himself looking directly into the man's eyes.

Beneath the brim of his cap, a silver stud was embedded in his left brow bone, with two piercing points positioned one above the other, catching the light with a sharp metallic glint.

Those eyes made the fat man shiver.

They were pale brown and narrow, with a faint grey tint around the centres of the irises. They looked almost like the eyes of some wild animal.

"I come here every day."

His expression remained blank, and his voice was soft.

"See you tomorrow?"

Taken out of context, the words could almost have sounded like a gentle invitation.

Zhou Huai came running back, panting, just in time to see the fat man limping out of the alley. He kept looking over his shoulder, trembling so badly he barely even noticed Zhou Huai.

Zhou Huai had been rather surprised by the sight. But when he stepped into the shop, he found something even more surprising.

"Hey! Isn't this the handsome mute guy who was helping sell sweet potatoes earlier?"

Qin Yiyu was giving a thumbs-up. At that, he burst out laughing.

"So it was you all along! Wait, seriously? Why does it always have to be you?"

"Huh?"

Something about that sounded off to Zhou Huai.

"Hold on. You two... know each other?"

"This is the guy I told you about. The one who showed up at my house and practically hunted me down."

With the person in question now standing right in the doorway, Qin Yiyu curled his lips into a smile and looked towards him.

"Nan Yi, right?"

It was a name that was hard to forget.

Zhou Huai's eyes widened. He silently mouthed at Qin Yiyu:

That girl you were talking about?

"Girl, my ass."

Qin Yiyu snatched up a tissue box from beside him and hurled it at Zhou Huai.

Nan Yi paid absolutely no attention to their exchange. He simply continued answering the last thing he'd said to him, his tone as calm as ever, as though the person who'd just beaten someone half to death hadn't been him.

"I came to see you. I just happened to help out."

Qin Yiyu had no intention of being so moved by a little act of heroism that he'd offer himself in return.

He stretched lazily.

"Well, thank you for stepping in when you saw someone being bullied. But I'm afraid I can't help you with what you're asking for. I already made that very clear last time."

Just thinking about their previous encounter gave him a headache.

It had been a long time since he'd met someone this difficult to deal with.

Every time the guy showed up, it was completely out of the blue. Last week, he'd practically given Qin Yiyu a heart attack.

He'd still been half-asleep that morning, forcing himself to get out of bed and open the door. And there he'd been: dressed in black, standing outside his apartment, wearing a cap just like today.

The corridor had been pitch-black. Qin Yiyu couldn't make out his features. The only thing he could see clearly was the bottle of alcohol in his hand, catching a faint glimmer of light.

His first thought had been:

"Come to collect a debt?"

"No."

The other man tossed the bottle back where he'd found it—a cardboard box outside the door used for rubbish—and brushed his hands together.

Qin Yiyu breathed a sigh of relief and patted his chest.

"Then why are you making that face? You're terrifying."

He hadn't been able to see the upper half of Nan Yi's face, but his reply had left a lasting impression.

"Born this way."

Nan Yi hadn't looked him in the eye. Instead, his gaze had settled on the tattoo at Qin Yiyu's throat, then travelled down to the tattoo on his wrist.

Only then had he abruptly introduced himself.

"My name is Nan Yi."

Qin Yiyu had been barely conscious that morning. Nan Yi had stood in front of him like a robot, delivering one statement after another, but Qin Yiyu hadn't really absorbed much of it.

He'd only remembered the name.

And the request that followed: he wanted to start a band with him.

Start a band?

That was somehow even worse than coming to collect a debt.

Qin Yiyu had laughed as though he'd just heard the funniest joke in the world.

"Just hearing the word 'band' makes me sick. Please, stop before I throw up on your shoes."

The guy had practically dug three feet underground to track him down and drag him out of hiding. He was completely insane.

Of course, Qin Yiyu had once had more than his fair share of obsessive fans.

There had been people waiting for him in his apartment complex's parking lot in the middle of the night, people banging on his hotel room door, and even people who'd stripped off their clothes backstage and thrown themselves at him.

There was no shortage of lunatics.

Later, after he'd been kicked out of his band, plenty of record labels and producers had gone to ridiculous lengths to sign him. They'd tried everything from threats to tempting offers. He couldn't escape them even if he wanted to.

Then there were the crazies who'd turned against him because of various rumours, following him around and throwing CDs from his old band straight at his face.

That was when he'd discovered something new: if you threw a CD hard enough, it could actually draw blood.

At the time, he'd wiped the blood from his forehead and marvelled:

"Fuck. These things are built to last."

Just hearing the word band was enough to bring all those unpleasant memories flooding back, fuelled by the alcohol still in his system.

So much time had passed, yet he felt like someone who'd died without quite making it to the other side. He was still stuck somewhere in the middle of the journey to the afterlife, forever waiting to drink the mythical Forgetting Potion that would wipe away his memories.

He'd wanted to forget every last miserable thing.

The more of that potion there was, the more he wanted to drink. But he'd gulped it down too quickly and ended up choking himself back to life.

Qin Yiyu nearly threw up for real.

Whenever he thought about bands, he should have remembered the hum of an electric guitar.

Instead, all he could hear in his head was the incessant wailing of ambulance sirens.

So he'd simply said:

"Stop showing up at my apartment. If you come again, I'll call the police."

Strangely enough, given how he'd expected things to go, Qin Yiyu had assumed the guy would keep pestering him for at least a few more days.

Every time he opened his front door after that, he'd mentally prepared himself for another encounter.

But the guy really hadn't come back.

After a few days, Qin Yiyu had started to think he'd finally accepted reality.

Who could've guessed he'd track him down at Zhou Huai's shop instead?

How on earth had he found this place?

Qin Yiyu was genuinely curious. The guy might as well become a spy. What was the point of trying to start a band?

"Could you come and take a look at our..."

Before Nan Yi could even finish saying rehearsal, Qin Yiyu cut him off without the slightest hesitation.

"No."

"Why?"

"Must you ask why about everything? That sounds exhausting."

Without even lifting his eyes, Qin Yiyu continued.

"If you've come all this way just for this, then let me make something clear. No matter how many times you come back, my answer will always be the same. I'm not doing it."

The two faced each other in silence.

As Qin Yiyu's friend for many years, Zhou Huai knew him well. After everything he'd been through, he was no longer the person he'd once been.

There might still be room to talk him around on other matters.

But getting him back into a band?

That was never going to happen. Not in this lifetime.

The summer heat had yet to fade. A warm late-season breeze swept a leaf into the shop, carrying it in a little spiral until it settled at Nan Yi's feet.

He lowered his eyes and glanced at it.

"What about something else?"

"Something else? You're quite demanding, aren't you?"

The careless smile never left Qin Yiyu's face. As usual, he began talking nonsense.

"Don't tell me you want me to give you a tattoo? Did you see that guy just now? That's one of my most loyal customers. I wouldn't want you coming back to crack my skull open like he did. That would break my heart."

Zhou Huai couldn't stand hearing someone insult his work.

"Hey, you little—"

"That wasn't your work."

Nan Yi spoke before he could finish, his tone certain.

Qin Yiyu's brow furrowed slightly.

"How do you know?"

Your drawing skills are worse than a kindergartener's. How could you possibly tattoo anyone?

Nan Yi didn't answer. Instead, he turned his head, his gaze passing over the rows of earrings displayed on the wall.

"Pierce my ears."

Qin Yiyu hadn't expected such a simple request.

"Sure. As long as you pay. Want to get it done now?"

"No."

"Then when?"

"Soon."

With that, Nan Yi turned and walked towards the door.

What was with all the cryptic answers?

"Hey. Don't come back here anymore. I won't be around here much longer, either."

But Nan Yi didn't say anything. He didn't even look back.

Qin Yiyu watched him disappear, remaining strangely still for a long moment.

For some reason, there was something familiar about his back. As though he'd seen him somewhere before.

But no matter how hard he tried, he couldn't remember where.

An even stranger thought crossed his mind.

He wanted to take that cap off Nan Yi's head and get a proper, thorough look at his face.

Just so I can figure out how to avoid him more effectively.

Qin Yiyu tried to give this inexplicable curiosity a perfectly reasonable explanation.

Of course, he never got the chance. Nan Yi had already left, quick and decisive as ever.

After all that commotion, the shop was a complete mess.

Zhou Huai sighed.

"Seriously, are you some kind of walking disaster? You've only been guarding the shop for a few days, and you've already attracted every freak and troublemaker in the neighbourhood. Anyway, weren't you teaching kids nursery rhymes earlier? Hurry up and go. My little shop can't afford to host someone as important as you."

"It's a music theory class, you idiot."

Qin Yiyu put the bottle of tattoo ink away.

"Didn't I tell you? The owner went back to his hometown a couple of days ago, so none of my classes were scheduled. He'll be back the day after tomorrow."

"Right, right."

For some reason, Zhou Huai suddenly remembered the letter.

He hesitated before speaking.

"By the way... Have those debt collectors come looking for you again these past few days?"

"No. I only moved a few days ago. They probably haven't tracked me down yet."

Qin Yiyu answered absentmindedly, his thoughts drifting back to that guy from earlier.

Actually, maybe he didn't need to become a spy after all. If he worked for a debt collection agency, he'd be employee of the month without breaking a sweat.

"Oh."

Zhou Huai swallowed the rest of his words, his neck stiffening.

Qin Yiyu noticed something was off.

"What's wrong?"

Zhou Huai avoided his gaze.

"Nothing. Just asking."

He bent down to sweep up the broken glass. Unexpectedly, he spotted a black cardholder in the corner. He picked it up, unzipped it to take a look, then tossed it straight into Qin Yiyu's arms.

"How does such a handsome guy manage to be so careless?"

Qin Yiyu caught it casually.

The cardholder looked rather old. It was black, with two white embroidered letters, NY, in the bottom-right corner.

Custom-made, too. Probably a gift from some girl.

He unzipped it and found several bank cards inside, along with a folded admission ticket for an English placement examination.

The name on the candidate's section read Nan Yi.

And the university listed beneath it was the one Qin Yiyu knew better than almost any other.

His own university.

The examination was scheduled for tomorrow morning.

Zhou Huai rested both hands on top of his broom handle and grinned.

"Well, would you look at that. You're university alumni."

Qin Yiyu couldn't be bothered to respond. He tucked the admission ticket back inside, zipped up the cardholder, and tossed it onto the counter before returning to his reclining chair for another nap.

"Looks pretty important. He might come back for it in a bit."

With his eyes half-closed, Qin Yiyu thought, Who knows?

The guy had said he'd come every day.

He'd even said, See you tomorrow.

But Qin Yiyu had also told him not to come back.

And Nan Yi looked like someone who kept his word.

Out of habit, Qin Yiyu squeezed his left hand, rolled over, and decided he couldn't be bothered thinking about it anymore.

Back at the university, Nan Yi parked his car and took his ID card and student card out of his pocket, intending to put them in the inner compartment of his backpack.

As he turned around, he happened to run into Chi Zhiyang.

His newly dyed white hair was impossible to miss.

Chi Zhiyang was sitting on the edge of a flower bed, looking as though he'd been waiting for ages. The moment their eyes met, he sprang to his feet and hurried over, his hair bouncing wildly, the little braid at the back swinging from side to side like a tail.

He knew Nan Yi had gone to see Qin Yiyu, so he'd skipped class to come and wait for him.

After all, there wasn't much time left. Things were practically at the eleventh hour.

When Nan Yi didn't say anything, Chi Zhiyang grew increasingly anxious.

"Did it work? What did he say? Does he still remember you?"

Three questions at once.

Nan Yi wasn't sure which one to answer first. Then again, the answers were more or less the same.

"No."

It hadn't worked.

Qin Yiyu hadn't said much.

And why would he remember him?

Nan Yi had never intended for Qin Yiyu to remember him in the first place.

Before he could dwell on his disappointment, Chi Zhiyang noticed him slipping his ID card into his backpack and frowned.

"Hey, where's your cardholder?"

He normally carried it with him everywhere.

Nan Yi slung his backpack over his shoulder.

"Lost it."

"Lost it?!"

That was the cardholder his grandmother had made for him by hand!

As his childhood friend, Chi Zhiyang was even more anxious about it than Nan Yi himself.

"What are we going to do, then? Do you remember where you lost it? Can we still find it?"

"Mm."

Nan Yi's tone was always steady.

"They'll bring it back." -->

